/**
 * Refuse an answers-request dispatch whose wait has already resolved.
 *
 * `answersRequest` claims the thread still waits for what this dispatch delivers.
 * Like every claim this module acts on, it is verified here rather than trusted: CI
 * can hold a validation for many minutes, and the thread it will land on is the one
 * that exists at the END of the wait, not the one the caller saw at the start (#283
 * — the reviewer a hand-off had already dispatched reviewed, returned the ball to a
 * person, and five minutes later the validation dispatched the same reviewer again
 * onto a thread that had stopped asking).
 *
 * ## The rule
 *
 * The thread's last turn event decides:
 *
 *   - `waiting` — the run that was asked for ended inside a child-starting tool and
 *     the node waits on CI or a child. This dispatch is the answer the node is still
 *     owed. It goes.
 *   - `handed-off` — the caller just wrote its own handoff and is dispatching the
 *     agent it named (the reload). It goes.
 *   - `returned` — the ball is with a person. Whoever it was for has been served;
 *     starting the same work again is the double review #283 reports.
 *   - `asked` — a request stands untaken. A run is queued or in flight on this node
 *     already, and a second dispatch would be a second run on one node — the same
 *     answer the outstanding-request check gives a marker dispatch.
 *   - no events at all — nothing in the thread has moved since the node was opened.
 *     There is no evidence the wait resolved, and the request this fulfils is the
 *     body's own line (`requestOutstandingOn` reads comments only for the same
 *     reason), so it goes.
